## Solution

Let $P(x) = x(x-1)(x-2)\cdots(x-2016) = \prod_{k=0}^{2016}(x-k)$, a polynomial of degree $2017$.

**Key observation: antisymmetry about the midpoint.**

Make the substitution $x = 2016 - t$:

$$P(2016-t) = \prod_{k=0}^{2016}(2016-t-k) = \prod_{k=0}^{2016}\bigl(-(t-(2016-k))\bigr) = (-1)^{2017}\prod_{k=0}^{2016}(t-(2016-k)).$$

As $k$ ranges over $\{0,1,\ldots,2016\}$, the value $2016-k$ also ranges over $\{0,1,\ldots,2016\}$ (just in reverse order). Hence

$$\prod_{k=0}^{2016}(t-(2016-k)) = \prod_{j=0}^{2016}(t-j) = P(t).$$

Since $2017$ is odd, $(-1)^{2017} = -1$, so

$$P(2016-t) = -P(t).$$

**Evaluating the integral.** Let $I = \int_0^{2016} P(x)\,dx$. Substitute $x = 2016 - t$, $dx = -dt$; the limits $x=0\to t=2016$ and $x=2016\to t=0$:

$$I = \int_{2016}^{0} P(2016-t)(-dt) = \int_0^{2016} P(2016-t)\,dt = \int_0^{2016} (-P(t))\,dt = -I.$$

Therefore $I = -I$, which forces $2I = 0$, i.e.

$$I = 0.$$

**Verification with a small case.** For $n=2$: $\int_0^2 x(x-1)(x-2)\,dx = \int_0^2 (x^3-3x^2+2x)\,dx = \bigl[\tfrac{x^4}{4}-x^3+x^2\bigr]_0^2 = 4-8+4 = 0$. ✓ The same antisymmetry argument applies whenever the number of factors is odd (degree odd), which holds here since there are $2017$ factors.

$$\boxed{\int_0^{2016} x(x-1)(x-2)\cdots(x-2016)\,dx = 0}$$

